{"title":"Matrix operation","markdown":{"headingText":"Matrix operation","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-05_matrix_op","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## Concepts and intutions {.unnumbered}\nJames  = Marie, James + Marie = Jamie.\nHere is an example of using mathematical notation.\nYou should know by now that the equal is not really equal in mathematics, and the addition is merely a notation.\nYet, they actually have a meaning.\nIn fact, James x Marie = Jarie is another notation and might be because they multiply.\n\nSo matrix is a rectangle, or a different kind of object at least in mathematics.\nAs far as we know we have numbers, they are also calles scalar and they are essentially a 1 x 1 matrix.\n\nWhat does it mean adding Matrix 1 + Matrix 2 or Matrix 1 per matrix 2. What is multiplication and addition\n\nIn this section, we do the simplest that is Matrix 1 is just a scalar. Or combining matrices with same dimension.\nRetain the original property of\n\nThe earliest might be with shopping.\nX bought 2 banana, 4 apples and 0 orange\nY bought x banana, 4 apples and 3 orange\nZ\nA\n\n## Formal {.unnumbered}\nAdding a scalar\nAdding a matrix M means M + 1 = M were each entry is added 1\n\nMultiplying a scalar means that M * 2 = where each entry is multiplied by 2\n\nAdding two matrices\nThis is a special case, and the simplest is matrix of same dimension, so that M + L = adding each entry\n\nMultiply two matrices (Hadamard product)\n\nThe advantage is that it is commutative\nM + 1 = 1 + M\nM * 2 = 2 * M\n\nIt is also associative\nM + L + K = M + K + L\n\nAnd distributive\n\nThis is a desired feature where the + and * have kept their original meaning.\n\n## Examples {.unnumbered}\nAssume we wanted to add one fruit for each person\n\nChange kg to g\n\nAdding only at certain cases\n\n## Tips and warning {.unnumbered}\nThese are well defined, essentially, define and do as it was defined.\nYou might run into issues, when you apply them in coding.\nThere are other, Kronecker product but essentially you come up with rules and define a symbol for it\nWe have determinant too.\nAlthough we could define subtraction in similar way, careful with division so that we don't divide by zero in any of the entry\n\n## if you want to know more {.unnumbered}\nLinear algebra","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":false,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"message":false,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","output-file":"chap05_matrix_op.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-l